A||N||20||87PegasusGT||Wheels, wheels||||5||
Z||000000||87PegasusGT||12-22-1999||03:28 PM||||I have 215/60 on my stock rear wheels<BR>(7.5'?) I want to put 245 or 255s on my rears<BR>To do so, I must obviously go to a wider rim.<BR>I like Mangels Series 77s. They are 15 x 9s<BR>and 15 x 7.5. Can I put the 15 x 9s on my rears, or will they fit? Any idea on what profile tire I should go with? Can I get 16"<BR>rims that would work better?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-22-1999||04:02 PM||||Depends on what the offset of the wheel is.  I believe the stock offset on an '87 was 34mm measured at the center of the wheel.  If the offset is the same, you can use the wheels, but with a wheel that is 9" wide, you're gonna stick way out.  There are alot of 16" and even 17" rims that will work.  There are basically 3 things you need to be concerned with:  1.  Offset-34mm (except for the rear of the '88s-they were 41mm, I think).  2.  Bolt pattern-5 x 100mm (4.5in, I think).  3.  Circumference-of course, this will depend on the tires.  You could go with a tire that will give you a greater circ., but it would throw your speedo off.||||reg||1
Z||000002||87PegasusGT||12-22-1999||04:05 PM||||Any suggestions on wider rims that will fit?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eddie||12-22-1999||06:05 PM||||CALL BF they have tires 245*50s that fit 14s on stock fieros I have them its no bull but hard to find.  If you call them they will help you finD them. I have 245*50*14 in the back and 205*60*14s up front and they are GREAT same size side walls with wider tires in the back do not let any one tell you some thing other then that!!!! they are the best set up with out changing your rims !!!!!I was going to change rims but some thing came up at home so I have note yet moved to the next level yet but I will some day soon I would like 245 *50*15s all the way around||||reg||6
Z||000004||william_thorniii||12-22-1999||06:05 PM||||I have a question... are you going with wider rims/tires for performance or looks?  I am only curious.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000005||william_thorniii||12-22-1999||06:20 PM||||Eddie,<P>I am curious... do you have a V-8 in that beast?  I can't imagine needing 9.5" tires on a Fiero.  I like aggressive looking Fiero's as much as the next guy, but tires being 2.65" wider than the rims that they are on?  Does this not compromise the tires durability, bead, etc, buy causing the sidewalls to bend in?  I love the way that wrinkle sidewall tires look on drag cars, but I think that they look out of place on 4 & 6 cylinder equipt vehicle.  This is of course my opininion and I am not flaming you, just wondering....<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000006||Patrick||12-22-1999||06:37 PM||||<BR>Eddie, are your rear rims 6 inches wide? If so, I would think that the <B>245/50/14</B> tires would be be kinda wide for those rims. From what I understand, the ideal combination is to have a rim the same width as the tread of the tire. This ensures that the tread of the tire actually lays flat on the ground. I believe with your combination the tire tread would not be flat, it would be be rounded. I think you'll find that your rear tires will be wearing out much faster towards the center of the tread.<P>Just out of curiousity, what is the width of the tread on those <B>245/50/14</B> tires? Is it about 7 1/2 inches?||||reg||5
Z||000007||87PegasusGT||12-22-1999||06:41 PM||||I curently am running NOS & a Chip, but I am doing the 335HP Intercooled 3.8l SC swap, so <BR>looks aside, I need the traction. Eddie: I have 15" rims not 14". Can I do the same thing?||||reg||1
Z||000008||87PegasusGT||12-22-1999||06:46 PM||||I also thought you needed to have equivalent tire/rim width so your tires don't "roll" when you are cornering. Is this true?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Patrick||12-22-1999||06:50 PM||||<BR>Makes sense to me... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000010||william_thorniii||12-22-1999||07:07 PM||||Patrick,<P>The easiest way to determine the english equivalent of tire size is to simply divide the number by 25.4.  The 245 is in millimeters and there are exactly 24.5 millimeters in 1 inch.  So 245mm = 9.64566...in.  <P>Generally you want your tire size to be similar to your wheel for the aforemention wear reasons.  Also, air pressure suspends your car from the road in conjunction with sidewall construction.  An exceptionally wide tire essentially eliminates the tire from helping to hold the car up.  Depending on the tires construction a set up like this is prone for early failure, usually in a catastrophic manner such as a blow out.  <P>I hope that this doesn't happen since it usually does considerable damage to both cars and individuals.  This is precisely why I questioned earlier why 245's were needed on the rear.  I understand that the original intention was to replace them with the wheels, but why would you have elected to put the tires on at a time when the wheels could not be afforded?  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000011||Patrick||12-22-1999||09:01 PM||||<BR>Thanks William, but I think you and I are talking apples and oranges here. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I believe what you've given me is the <B>section width</B> of the tire and not the <B>tread width</B>. The <B>section width</B> is the "bulge" between the sidewalls. It is always wider than the <B>tread width</B>.<P>The <B>tread width</B> can vary from manufacturer to manufacturer with the same tire size, in this case a 245/50/14. So I still need Eddie to get his tape measure out. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||14
