Z||000020||theogre||12-24-1999||08:11 PM||||Patrick,<P>If you want the tread width of a tire you need to look up the specific tire. tread width for a given size of tire can varry considerably from one tire design to the next. Don't use the tire size as an estimate. The next bigger tire can actually have a narrower tread than the tire you replace. For example if you check 195/70R14 across sveral tires and a couple brands you find a tread width variation of over an inch.<P>Others,<P>A wider rim will need a diferant offset. Not the same as the OEMs. Some of you are mixing up backset and offset.<P>Backset is the simple measurement of the dpth of the wheel mounting surface from the back of the wheel.<P>Offset is the distance "off center" that the hub face is relative to the center of the tire.<P>On a wider wheel you need the backset the same or the wheel will contact the spring rack on the strut. Offset will change inward as the wheel gets wider to acomplish that.<P>Also when you look up tire sizes on the manufacturor sites, check the reference rim size. This is the rim that the tire size was meared on. There will also be a range of rim sizes listed. This range is what the tire will mount properly on. Ideally tho you want your rim within .5 inch +- of the reference rim so that the tire will retain it's designed profile.||||reg||1
